WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
Thanks for joining me on Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 135.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.900
Today is July 18th, 2026.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.740
Some fascinating developments to cover today.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.640
A private Indian rocket just reached orbit on its first try

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.820
while a NASA probe wakes up billions of miles out.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.340
Vikram won, lifted off on its inaugural flight July 18th

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.800
and reached orbit successfully.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.520
The launch marks the first time a commercial Indian rocket

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.380
has achieved orbital insertion.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.420
Skyroot Aerospace developed the vehicle

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.620
as India's initial private orbital class launcher,

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.980
with former ISRO engineers on the team.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.660
The success opens the path for additional commercial missions

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.560
from the country, including future payload deployments.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.120
The vehicle completed its first flight

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.920
without prior orbital attempts.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.240
Skyroot now holds both a launch license

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.840
and demonstrated hardware that can be iterated

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.780
for future missions.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.020
Next steps include payload integration for follow-on flights

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.300
and potential scaling of the design to larger variants.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.780
While one new launcher joins the orbital club,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.400
another established deep space mission is stirring back to life

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.640
after a long sleep.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.100
The new horizon spacecraft ended nearly a year of hibernation

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.100
at a distance of nearly six billion miles from Earth.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.520
It is currently positioned in the outer solar system

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.900
beyond Pluto's orbit.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.640
Mission controllers will use the wake period

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.280
for new observations of the outer solar system,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.720
including distant, kuiper belt objects.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.000
The spacecraft continues its extended mission

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.000
with updated instruments after the long dormancy.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.680
As new horizons resumes work in the outer darkness,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.420
closer to home, another comet is putting on a visible show.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.100
Comet 10P temple approaches Perihelian in early August

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.060
after traveling inward from the outer solar system.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.620
Earth is passing through the plane of the comet's orbit,

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.800
producing visible changes in the tail orientation.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.400
The comet now displays a knife-edge tail feature

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.280
as it nears the sun with dust and gas structures

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.480
becoming more pronounced.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.920
Observers can track these shifts through the coming weeks

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.580
using ground-based telescopes.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.900
If you shrank the solar system so the sun sat

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.600
at one end of a football field,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.880
a typical comet nucleus would be smaller than a grain

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.920
of sand at the opposite goal line.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.740
That tiny body spends most of its time

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.140
in the distant, orat cloud, or kuiper belt

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.940
until a gravitational nudge sends it inward.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.260
As it crosses roughly five AU from the sun,

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.160
sunlight begins to warm its surface ices,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.300
releasing gas and dust that form two distinct tails.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.220
One tail is pushed almost straight back by radiation pressure

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.640
while the other curves under the influence of the solar wind.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.360
At peak activity near Perihelian,

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.600
the comet can shed thousands of tons of material per day,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.700
yet the nucleus itself may be only a few kilometers across.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.240
The exact balance between sublimation rate, dust grain size,

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.680
and magnetic draping that sculpts the observed tail structures

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.480
remains only partly understood.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.660
While new worlds are being spotted from the ground,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.680
two long-running missions at Jupiter continue to map

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.360
the giant planet and its moons.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.480
The world is the faintest alien planet

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.440
yet photographed from Earth using current ground-based facilities.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.340
Direct imaging required advanced techniques,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.020
including adaptive optics,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.940
to separate the planet's light from its host star.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.340
The detection demonstrates improving capability

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:36.440
to observe dim companions at large separations

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.280
from their stars.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.520
Follow-up observations are planned to characterize

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.740
the object further through spectroscopy.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.360
Juno remains active at Jupiter collecting data on the planet

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.560
and its moons through ongoing orbits.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.500
Europa Clipper is scheduled to reach the system in 2030

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.140
for focus study of the icy moon Europa.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.820
Both missions target the solar system's largest planet

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.920
and its satellite system with complementary instruments.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.280
The combined effort supports ongoing investigation

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.320
of Jupiter's environment, including magnetic fields

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.020
and atmospheric dynamics.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.140
In contrast to steady progress at Jupiter,

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.420
Russia's planned reusable rocket keeps slipping.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.740
Six years after its announcement,

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.600
the reusable AMER rocket continues to slip year for year

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.220
according to project timelines.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.380
The vehicle was positioned as Russia's answer

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.420
to the Falcon 9 with plans for first-stage reuse.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.320
Development setbacks have kept the project

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.040
from advancing to hardware testing or flight attempts.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.560
Observer's note, the timeline has stretched consistently

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.200
since the initial reveal without visible progress

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.340
toward static fires or integrated vehicle tests.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.420
On a more positive private industry note,

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.760
a US company just received approval

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.360
for an unusual orbital reflector test.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.800
Reflect orbital received approval

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.200
for the initial year Endill satellite under the new license.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.700
The constellation aims to demonstrate space-based

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.180
reflectors capable of redirecting sunlight.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.140
Astronomical groups have raised concerns

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.360
about increased sky brightness affecting observations.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.600
The company plans to proceed with the first launch

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.000
under the granted license while addressing community feedback.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.560
The partnership between ISRO and SCTIMST

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.220
will examine human physiological responses to space flight

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:29.420
including cardiovascular changes.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.100
Work focuses on medical and biological aspects

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.200
of long duration missions beyond low-earth orbit.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.040
The collaboration draws on ISRO's flight experience

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.360
and SCTIMST's clinical expertise in cardiology and neurology.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.080
Initial studies target cardiovascular

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.040
and other health factors relevant to astronauts

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.600
during extended stays.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.420
The Apollo lunar lander placed astronauts on the moon

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:00.660
during the program's flights between 1969 and 1972.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.520
The feat remains unmatched in scale

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.000
by any other space program in terms of crude landings.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.380
The article reviews the engineering

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.660
and operational challenges overcome during those missions

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.800
including descent propulsion and guidance systems.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.520
It positions the landings as a benchmark

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.580
for human space flight that later programs continue

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:22.260
to reference.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.240
Development delays led to termination

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.680
of the planned lunar lander effort after years of work.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.240
The spacecraft had been intended to deliver cargo

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.460
to the moon under a NASA contract.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.800
NASA and Draper Laboratory jointly decided

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.540
to cancel the project following missed milestones.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.440
Resources will shift to other elements

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.140
of the lunar architecture including alternative lander providers.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.080
The suite 15 experiments subjected a 15-foot wing

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:52.800
to loads beyond design limits

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.400
at NASA's Armstrong Flight Research Center.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.700
Researchers recorded structural behavior

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.160
under extreme conditions including bending

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.380
and torsion forces.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.720
The thin, braced configuration showed promise

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.640
for future aircraft efficiency through reduced weight.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.240
Data will inform scaling decisions

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.400
for larger flight articles in potential

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.860
supersonic or subsonic applications.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.220
The 570 megapixel camera captured the corona

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.760
Australis molecular cloud in high resolution detail.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.600
The region ranks among the closest star forming areas

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.400
to Earth at roughly 430 light years.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.740
The new image reveals intricate dust structures

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.280
resembling brushstrokes across the field.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.520
Astronomers will use the data to study early stellar

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.620
formation processes within the cloud's dense cores.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.740
Images taken between May 2nd and May 15th

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.780
show Mars increasing in apparent size

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.280
from a small crescent to a larger disk.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.760
The spacecraft used the planet

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.020
for a gravity assist maneuver to adjust its trajectory

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.500
toward the asteroid belt.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.600
The sequence documents the approach geometry

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.980
during the flyby with multiple frames

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.520
from the onboard imager.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.780
Psychic continues toward its main asteroid target

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:09.860
after the encounter.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.740
The orbiter recorded wave-like features

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.100
with a metallic appearance

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.560
in a previously lower resolution region.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.620
The dunes sit in a mid-latitude area

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.560
where wind patterns interact with surface materials.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.680
Scientists are examining composition and formation mechanisms

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.800
behind the patterns using infrared and visible data.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:33.700
Additional orbital passes will help refine surface models

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.480
and identify mineral content.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.580
The observatories measured the magnetic field

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:44.500
around pulsar PSR J1101 minus 6101 for the first time

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.720
using combined x-ray observations.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.620
x-ray data in multiple bands reveal field structure

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.200
within the nebula surrounding the rapidly spinning neutron star.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.140
The combined image shows how the pulsar's emission interacts

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.120
with surrounding material through synchrotron processes.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.300
Further analysis will constrain particle acceleration models

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.160
in the nebula.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.880
New missions and fresh data keep the frontier moving every week.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.020
Keep an eye on the first orbital reflector launch

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.860
as the company moves forward with initial hardware

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.160
under the new approval.

